---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Grant research software for nonprofits"
  description="The grant research tools worth paying for — Instrumentl, GrantStation, Candid, Grants.gov — compared by price and shop size, with what each actually does."
  section="grant-research">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Grant research' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Grant research software</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Grant research tools answer one question: which funders are worth your
        limited hours? The credible options run from free (Grants.gov) to about
        $200 a year (GrantStation via TechSoup) to $2,000+ a year (Instrumentl) —
        and the right answer depends almost entirely on how many grants your shop
        pursues.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        Start with volume. If you submit a handful of applications a year to
        funders you already know, a paid database mostly confirms what you know —
        Grants.gov plus free library access to Candid covers you. Once
        prospecting becomes a recurring job — you're hunting new funders every
        month, tracking a dozen deadlines, managing renewals — a paid tool starts
        paying for itself in hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical ladder most shops climb: <strong>Grants.gov</strong> (free,
        federal only) → <strong>GrantStation</strong> (~$199/yr through TechSoup —
        a broad database you filter yourself) → <strong>Instrumentl</strong>
        (from $179/mo — active matching, deadline tracking, and 990 data in one
        place, priced like the productivity tool it is). <strong>Candid's
        Foundation Directory</strong> sits alongside as the research standard for
        deep 990 diligence, and many public libraries offer it free.
      </p>

      <h2>The one mistake to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Don't buy the expensive tool to fix a pipeline problem. A database finds
        funders; it doesn't write the proposals or build the funder
        relationships. If your last five applications died from lack of writing
        time, more prospects will not help. Buy research software when
        <em>finding</em> funders is the bottleneck — it's the best money you'll
        spend — and not before.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="grant-research" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        See where grant research fits in <a href="/stacks/250k-1m/">the $250k–$1M
        stack</a> (first paid tool) and <a href="/stacks/1m-5m/">the $1M–$5M
        stack</a> (team-scale).
      </p>
      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
